# The Android 10 wpa_supplicant control socket, made to exist and then bridged.
#
# THE PROBLEM, in two halves.
#
# (1) On Android 10 wpa_supplicant creates NO per-interface control socket at
#     all. ctrl_iface_unix.c:718-752 compares the tail of the global control
#     interface name "@android:wpa_wlan0" with conf->ctrl_interface, which
#     /vendor/etc/wifi/wpa_supplicant.conf sets to "wlan0"; they match, and the
#     function returns early having opened nothing. Measured live on this
#     handset with Wi-Fi up: /data/vendor/wifi/wpa/sockets and
#     /data/misc/wifi/sockets were both empty. Stock has the same defect.
#
# (2) Three MAL entity libraries open that socket by an absolute path that was
#     correct up to Android 8 and does not exist on Q:
#
#       libmal_epdga.so   /data/misc/wifi/sockets/wlan0  + .../ea_ctrlconn
#       libmal_rds.so     /data/misc/wifi/sockets/wlan0  + .../rds_ctrlconn
#       libmal_nwmngr.so  /data/misc/wifi/sockets/wlan0  + .../nwmngr_ctrlconn
#
#     Q's path is /data/vendor/wifi/wpa/sockets, compiled in at
#     external/wpa_supplicant_8/wpa_supplicant/Android.mk:72-73, and it is
#     LONGER than the old one -- so it can be changed neither from a device
#     tree nor by patching the strings in the blobs.
#
# THE FIX, also in two halves, and neither works alone.
#   init.wmt.rc passes -O/data/vendor/wifi/wpa/sockets, which makes
#   conf->ctrl_interface a path rather than "wlan0", so `same` is 0 and the
#   socket is created.
#   init.mt6755.rc bind-mounts that directory onto /data/misc/wifi/sockets, so
#   the blobs find it under the name they were built with.
#
# WHY THE VENDOR DIRECTORY AND NOT THE LEGACY ONE. /data/misc/wifi/sockets is
# wpa_socket, a core_data_file_type (system/sepolicy/public/file.te:435).
# Letting a vendor HAL create files there is exactly the Treble violation
# Google removed in device/google/marlin 66da37914e91, and it would need
# hal_wifi_supplicant_default added to data_between_core_and_vendor_violators.
# /data/vendor/wifi/wpa is wpa_data_file, a VENDOR type
# (system/sepolicy/vendor/file.te:2) that the supplicant may already write
# (vendor/hal_wifi_supplicant_default.te:19-21). Bind-mounting the vendor
# directory onto the legacy name gives the blobs their path while the inode --
# and therefore the SELinux label -- stays vendor. Nothing below is a
# relaxation of AOSP's hardening.
#
# WHAT MAL SENDS. STATUS (libmal_epdga), SIGNAL_POLL (libmal_rds) and
# BSS <bssid> (libmal_nwmngr). Three read-only queries; no SCAN, no DISCONNECT,
# no SET, nothing that could fight WifiService for the supplicant.
#
# DAC is already satisfied: the directory is 0770 wifi:wifi and mtkmal runs as
# uid 1001 with AID_WIFI (1010) among its supplementary groups -- read off
# /proc/<pid>/status on the handset, not assumed.
#
# STATUS: this is the one part of the VoWiFi chain that has never run, on this
# port OR on Stock. If Wi-Fi misbehaves after a flash, drop the -O in
# init.wmt.rc, the mount in init.mt6755.rc and this file; nothing else changes.

# The control connection is AF_UNIX SOCK_DGRAM in both directions: the client
# binds its own <name>_ctrlconn node in the same directory and sendto()s the
# server's node, and the server replies to the client's node.
allow mtkmal wpa_data_file:dir { add_name remove_name search write };
allow mtkmal wpa_data_file:sock_file { create unlink write };
allow mtkmal hal_wifi_supplicant_default:unix_dgram_socket sendto;
allow hal_wifi_supplicant_default mtkmal:unix_dgram_socket sendto;

# hal_wifi_supplicant_default is a halserverdomain, so check anything added
# here against system/sepolicy/public/hal_neverallows.te first: that file
# forbids halserverdomain { tcp_socket udp_socket rawip_socket } and
# { net_admin net_raw } outside a short exempt list. unix_dgram_socket is in
# neither neverallow, and hal_wifi_supplicant_server is on the exempt list for
# both anyway.
